   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "text": "How context flows\n• The KV cache IS the model's memory of the sequence. Each layer's attention output (the new context) is added to the residual stream,\n  and the next layer projects that enriched stream into its own K,V — so deeper caches hold more contextualised tokens.\n• GQA/MQA keep all 32 query heads (32 questions) and cut only the number of K/V 'lenses' on the past. MQA = one lens for all.\n• MLA stores one small latent per token; each head up-projects its own K,V from it (absorbed into W_q / W_o at inference) → near-MHA diversity.\n• Fewer KV heads ≠ shorter context: every past token is still attended. Windows / eviction (SWA, H2O, SnapKV) DO drop tokens from reach.\n• Decode reads the whole cache every step: bytes/step ≈ weights + KV/token × t. GQA-8's 4× smaller cache ≈ 4× less KV traffic → better TPOT.",
   "originalText": "How context flows\n• The KV cache IS the model's memory of the sequence. Each layer's attention output (the new context) is added to the residual stream,\n  and the next layer projects that enriched stream into its own K,V — so deeper caches hold more contextualised tokens.\n• GQA/MQA keep all 32 query heads (32 questions) and cut only the number of K/V 'lenses' on the past. MQA = one lens for all.\n• MLA stores one small latent per token; each head up-projects its own K,V from it (absorbed into W_q / W_o at inference) → near-MHA diversity.\n• Fewer KV heads ≠ shorter context: every past token is still attended. Windows / eviction (SWA, H2O, SnapKV) DO drop tokens from reach.\n• Decode reads the whole cache every step: bytes/step ≈ weights + KV/token × t. GQA-8's 4× smaller cache ≈ 4× less KV traffic → better TPOT.",
   "fontSize": 17,
   "fontFamily": 1,
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "middle",
   "containerId": "e125",
   "lineHeight": 1.25,
   "autoResize": true
  }
